; THE FIRST OROBOROS PROGRAM THAT OPENS A FILE.
;
; It exists as the acceptance test for one field in a data format. `Prim.Result`
; was a single Go string, so a target could not declare a host call that gives
; back several values — and `(T, error)` is Go's constructor idiom, so that one
; field refused **19.8% of Go's callable standard library**, more than every
; language-level limitation combined (gostdlib-2026-09-06 §4a). It is why this
; language had no file I/O, and the reason was never a design position.
;
; The LANGUAGE needed nothing. `(values a b)` has been the negative product since
; values.md, measured at 0.99x on Go with zero allocations, and it is eliminated
; by applying it to a continuation — which is what happens here. When the
; producer is a `def`, beta performs that application and the product vanishes;
; when it is a `prim`, beta cannot, so the shape reaches the backend intact and
; the backend emits the host's own form.
;
;   src, err := os.ReadFile("go.mod")
;
; No product is built on Go, because Go has several results natively. That is
; "emit at the highest layer the target natively provides" at a call boundary.

(use os)
(use io)

(export main)

; THE INPUT LIMIT IS STATED, and that is what makes the program provable rather
; than trapped. A line count accumulates over the input and a file is unbounded,
; so nothing can bound `(+ lines 1)` while nothing bounds the file — and the
; answer is not a bigger integer, it is saying how large a file this tool
; accepts. jsonfmt and freq say the same thing for the same reason, and
; `tree.oro`'s node cap said it first: a capacity does not create the limit, it
; makes it VISIBLE.
(def cap-in 16777216)

; Count the newlines in a file, named on the command line.
;
; `seq` ON THE ERROR BRANCHES IS THE ONE THING THIS PROGRAM SAYS ABOUT THE
; LANGUAGE. A conditional's branches must agree, and printing a message gives
; back a string where counting gives back an int — so the messages are sequenced
; and the value is stated. What is really missing is int-to-decimal as a library:
; `examples/big/render.oro` shows it is twenty lines of the language, but until
; it is a `lib/` module a program cannot turn its own answer into text, which is
; why `print-int` exists beside `print-line` at all.
;
; AND THE SECOND GUARD CANNOT FIRE. A file under the cap has fewer than that
; many newlines, so `(< lines cap-in)` is true at every iteration — it is there
; because the ANALYSIS needs it, not the program: a counter incremented under a
; condition has no bound from the loop's own guard, and `(+ lines 1)` was the one
; operation of two this program could not prove. tree.oro's node cap, jsonfmt's
; document cap and freq's derived word cap are all this same move.
;
; It is the OUTER test, and that is not a style choice: `(and a b)` erases to
; `(if a b false)` (ADR 0017), so writing the two conditions as one conjunction
; makes the enclosing `if`'s condition a nested `if` rather than a comparison,
; and the refinement layer reads facts off comparisons.
;
; The byte range is DECLARED on `ReadFile`, so `src` is a `[]byte` on Go, a
; packed array on V8 and a `short[]` on the JVM — where a file byte does not fit
; a signed `byte` — and `(src i)` may be compared without a refusal.
(def main ()
  (let av (os.Args)
    (if (< (len av) 2)
        (seq (io.print-line "usage: wc FILE") 0)
        ; SEVERAL RESULTS ARE BOUND, not passed to a continuation: `(tuple src
        ; err)` on the left of a `let` is the product's eliminator written in
        ; binding order (spec/binding.md §5), and it is the same term either way.
        (let (tuple src err) (os.ReadFile (av 1))
          (if (os.err-nil err)
              (if (>= (len src) cap-in)
                  (seq (io.print-line "wc: file is larger than this tool accepts") 0)
                  (loop ((i 0) (lines 0))
                    (>= i (len src)) (io.print-int lines)
                    else (again (+ i 1)
                          (if (< lines cap-in)
                              (if (= (src i) 10) (+ lines 1) lines)
                              lines))))
              (seq (io.print-line "wc: cannot read that file") 0))))))
